course: dictionary
lesson: 6232
title: A bird from a faraway land
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A moa is a noun, which means it is a thing, here a kind of bird. The first sentence gives you its meaning: «Moas were giant plant-eating birds of New Zealand.» The words giant, plant-eating and New Zealand are its context, and they tell you what kind of bird it is.
explain: Context also tells you what happened to a word's thing. «People arrived in New Zealand around 1300 and hunted the moas» and then «the moas died out within about 200 years». Died out is the clue: extinct means no longer alive anywhere in the world.
explain: Some phrases come from one place only. «Gone the way of the moa» is a New Zealand phrase: it does not mean a bird went anywhere, it means something has gone for ever. When you meet a phrase like this, ask whether the words make sense on their own. If not, look for the clues around it.
fact: A moa was a very large flightless bird that lived only in New Zealand.
fact: «Moa» is a noun, so you can say a moa, or two moas.
fact: «Extinct» means no longer alive anywhere in the world.
fact: The tallest moa stood taller than a person when reaching up.
fact: «Gone the way of the moa» is a New Zealand phrase meaning gone for ever.
fact: Haast's eagle was the biggest eagle ever known, and it hunted moa.
exercise: «Moas were giant plant-eating birds of New Zealand.» What does moa mean? | A small bird that could fly well | A kind of New Zealand tree | *A very large flightless bird from New Zealand | A type of eagle
exercise: «All moa are now extinct.» What does extinct mean? | Hidden away in a cave | *No longer alive anywhere | Very rare but still living in the forest | Very old and fragile
exercise: «Moas were giant plant-eating birds of New Zealand.» Which word tells you what moas ate? | giant | moas | birds | *plant-eating
test: The word flightless is in «a very large flightless bird». What does the word flightless suggest? | It had a long flight every year | *It could not fly | It lived in a cave | It could fly very fast
test: In New Zealand, what does «gone the way of the moa» mean? | Found again in a museum | Hunting in the forest | *Gone for ever | Gone to the shops
exam: Is moa a noun, a verb, an adjective or an adverb? | *noun | adverb | adjective | verb
